# Python bytecode — builders are run in place from the repo, so these appear
# next to the sources. They are build artifacts, never content.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# The virtual environment. requirements.txt is the tracked half; the installed
# tree is per-machine and per-platform.
.venv/

# Credentials. This repo is PUBLIC and jsDelivr serves it, so anything
# committed here is fetchable at a URL by anyone who guesses the path.
# Keys live in the FMP_API_KEY environment variable, or in these files, which
# never leave the machine. Selected by ENVIRONMENT (dev | prod) — no default.
# No exception, and no template file to make one necessary: nothing matching
# this pattern is ever trackable. The shape is documented in README.md instead,
# because `git.commit&push.bat` runs `git add .` — a negation here is one
# mis-ordered line away from publishing a key.
secrets.*.json

# Which environment this checkout runs as (ENVIRONMENT=dev|prod). Machine-local
# by definition: a clone on another machine, or a CI runner, decides for itself.
# It holds no secret — but tracking it would make one checkout's choice the
# repository's default, which is the assumption this project refuses to make.
.env

# Generated reports land beside the scenario that produced them. A report is a
# build artifact — the controller and the view are the source, and regenerating
# from the same symbol gives the same file with newer numbers. The scenario
# (.md) is tracked; its output carries live market data and would churn on
# every run, so it is not.
skills_testing_scenarios/**/*.html




